v365f5: [OBJ-ID] RECYCLE_OBJ_NUMBERS = False - retire Number recycling (object-id plan, A).
      Zero-risk single-flag change. Object Numbers are now strictly monotonic within a
      session; a freed Number is NEVER re-issued (the pool/quarantine code stays in place
      but dormant, so revert is one flag).
      WHY: recycling began as a v-line EXPERIMENT to hunt the mid-flight spawn freeze by
      shrinking the live Number set. That freeze is now fixed at its true root (the missing
      parachuter ServerConfirm, v361f5), so the premise is gone and only the downside
      remained. Recycling is the SUPPLY side of the object-overwrite / ghosting class: even
      with v324's FIFO+quarantine, an internet peer that missed a delete (e.g. a wrapped
      DELETE swallowed before v364f5) can still hold an old Number when the 300s quarantine
      lapses and it is handed to a different plane on a different station -> two aircraft
      share one Number -> wrong-plane / no-damage / ghost sightings, and the St-orphan
      NET::OBJECT[garbage] CTD. The v324 comment already did the math that makes recycling
      unnecessary: 0x0100..0xFFFF = 65,279 ids, ~120 spawns/session => hundreds of sessions
      before wrap. Not reusing within a session therefore costs nothing.
      RE CONTEXT: the server is ALREADY authoritative over Number - the client proposes only
      a local `ident` (per-client counter) in its out-4 and BLOCKS on the msg-5 ServerConfirm
      (FUN_007e5030 asserts NumberVar==-1 then writes the server's Number) before its sim
      loop starts. So the overwrite bug was never clients choosing ids; it was id LIFECYCLE.
      A (this change) removes reuse-collision. B (next) makes St / client_number
      server-authoritative and reservation-based to close the two-objects-on-one-station
      window that remains when a bail keeps a station while a new plane takes it.
      FALSIFICATION: with recycling off, a full field session must show object Numbers only
      ever INCREASING (watch CREATE2/CONFIRM5 lines - no Number should appear twice), and
      the wrong-plane / no-damage / ghosting reports tied to Number reuse should not recur.
      If ghosting persists with strictly-increasing Numbers, the remaining cause is St reuse
      (mechanism 1), which is exactly what B targets - a clean separation of the two.
