v357f5: [PERF] PERFORMANCE-ONLY BUILD, shipped as server-fable5.py (server.py stays v356).
      Built by make_fable5.py: 28 exact-text edits, each required to match exactly once in
      the v356 source, py_compile-verified before write. `python make_fable5.py --show`
      prints every edit. NO logic-bug fixes in this pass - those come after this build is
      proven not-broken in a live session. Three changes, all with revert flags:

      (1) RELIABLE-ACK 10ms DELAY MOVED OFF THE RX THREAD (_ack_sender_loop, ACK_ASYNC).
      The RX path did `time.sleep(0.01)` inline before every reliable ACK - the ONE thread
      that also serves telemetry relay, time pings and inbound ACK matching slept 10ms per
      reliable message from ANY client. DAMAGE28 rides the reliable channel, so the cost
      peaked exactly mid-combat, and a burst from one client delayed every player's relay.
      The 10ms pacing itself is PRESERVED per-packet (suspected client-side race: on LAN a
      sub-ms ACK can arrive before the client's send path finishes registering the packet
      in its reliable queue -> ACK lookup miss -> permanent retransmit). ACKs are queued to
      a dedicated sender thread; the queue is FIFO with a constant per-entry delay, so ACK
      ORDER is identical to the old code and every packet still gets >=10ms of grace from
      its own arrival. ONE KNOWN DIFFERENCE, stated plainly: a BURST of reliable packets
      now gets its ACKs back-to-back ~10ms after each arrival, where the old inline sleep
      also serialised them 10ms APART. If the client turns out to need the spacing (not
      just the delay), ACK_ASYNC=False restores the v356 inline path verbatim.

      (2) SEND POOL FOR BROADCAST FAN-OUT (_submit_send, SEND_POOL, 32 workers).
      This is the v328 "wants a small worker pool ... left for its own pass" item. The 22
      per-recipient fan-out LOOPS (chat x2, roster 62/63, scene 30/36/42, production
      40/71, DAMAGE28 relay, teardown deletes, ace-88, system msgs, arena counts, room
      echoes, UI add/remove, parachuter deletes, relay create-object) now submit to a
      fixed ThreadPoolExecutor instead of spawning one OS thread per recipient per
      message (v328 measured ~200 threads / ~23ms per chat line at 200 players). Each
      task runs the same send_rel call and blocks on the ACK exactly as before; pool
      execution is FIFO, which is MORE ordered than free thread scheduling was.
      Single-recipient echo sites (one thread per command, not O(N)) are DELIBERATELY
      untouched - identical cost to v356, zero behavioural risk. SEND_POOL=False (or any
      pool failure) falls back to thread-per-message.
      SIZING NOTE: 32 workers each blocking up to 3s on a dead client's ACK timeout means
      a few unreachable clients can briefly serialise a large broadcast; on LAN ACKs
      return in ms so the pool drains instantly. Raise SEND_POOL_WORKERS if arenas grow.

      (3) SQLITE WAL (init_db). journal_mode=WAL is persistent in the DB file, so all
      per-call db_* connections and the web admin inherit it - readers stop blocking
      writers, commit cost drops. Creates fa_server.db-wal/-shm alongside the DB (normal).
      Revert: PRAGMA journal_mode=DELETE.

      FALSIFICATION - CHECK THIS FIRST (v353 discipline): a clean run must show RELRX DUP
      counts comparable to v356, zero STALL-WATCH warnings, and no '[x] has left' lines
      that v356 would not have produced. Any of those -> flip ACK_ASYNC / SEND_POOL to
      False individually and re-test to isolate which change is at fault before touching
      anything else. The two flags are independent by design.

