v355: [WIRE + SESSION] two fixes: malformed DAMAGE28 frames are no longer relayed, and the
      HQ-exit re-join teardown is back with a guard that actually distinguishes a re-join.

  (1) [WIRE/CRITICAL] msg 28 RECORD-STRUCTURE VALIDATION.
      LAYOUT, established from the 9 real frames v352 captured (bc 0..15, 19B..244B) - every
      one fits exactly:
          [bc][T][00][00][1c][..][..][..][..][count]  then count x 9-byte records
          size == 10 + count*9,   count at byte[9]
        19B->1  28B->2  46B->4  64B->6  73B->7  91B->9  109B->11  226B->24  244B->26
      The CTD run (server_9_) relayed 46, 100, 19, 68, 19 bytes. All integral against that
      formula EXCEPT 68: (68-10)/9 = 6.444. That is the malformed frame. Relaying it desyncs
      the recipient's record walk, the remaining-length counter runs negative, and the client
      dies on `Length=-19` at Engine\Net\Network.cpp:249 - or, when the garbage is dereferenced
      before any bounds check catches it, `Access violation at net, v.addr:0xDEB800EB` at
      FA30\Main.cpp:2407. Same root, two exits, which is why the module varied.
      FIX: refuse to relay unless (len-10) %% 9 == 0 and byte[9] == (len-10)//9.

      CORRECTION TO AN EARLIER CALL: this is NOT a bc-ceiling problem like v351, and I said it
      was. bc runs to 15 (244B, 26 records) with no harm - the BIG frames are fine and the
      SMALL ones crashed the arena. Do not port TELEM_MAX_BC's shape to msg 28.
      Note also that v352 reported every frame as header-MATCH: it only checked bc/T against the
      overall length, and the count field was never cross-checked. A frame can be
      header-consistent and still be structurally malformed. Both must agree.

  (2) [SESSION] RE-JOIN TEARDOWN, SECOND ATTEMPT - keyed on _presence_advertised.
      v353 tried `entered_game and current_room is not None` and it fired on FIRST joins,
      because both are already set by the time slot assignment runs (v354 disabled it).
      The correct signal is "peers were actually TOLD about this player and never told to drop
      him". _presence_advertised is set in broadcast_player_join_62 AFTER the msg-62 goes out
      (so it means "told", not "about to tell"), and cleared by teardown_ingame_presence and by
      the msg-64 leave path.
      THE BUG (server_10_, ALL41_MAD): exit to HQ to change aircraft, then re-join. That route
      passes through neither the msg-64 leave (v182) nor the reap (v348), so peers kept his
      REMOTE_PLAYER and his stale objects while he took the SAME slot back. The msg-62 add then
      landed on an occupied index - the client's "Add existing REMOTE_PLAYER" no-op - and
      neither side rebuilt. He lost visual of every aircraft; nobody could see him.

      FALSIFICATION - CHECK THIS FIRST: [TEARDOWN] must NOT appear on a FIRST join. If it does,
      _presence_advertised is being set too early and this must be disabled again exactly as
      v353 was, because a spurious REMOVE for an ARRIVING player causes the very invisibility it
      is meant to fix - for everyone, on every join.
      Deadlock constraint unchanged: the call sits BEFORE the `with sl:` block, because
      teardown_ingame_presence -> broadcast_object_delete_3 -> get_sessions_in_room takes `sl`,
      a plain Lock rather than an RLock.

