v354: [REVERT] v353's teardown-on-slot-assignment is DISABLED. The guard was wrong and it
      fired on FIRST joins.

      v353 assumed entered_game/current_room are only True once a session is genuinely in the
      arena, so finding them set at slot-assignment time would mean stale presence from a
      previous life. They are ALREADY SET by that point in a NORMAL join.
      PROOF (run_20260727_184141, clean local test, nothing else running):
        18:41:41.185 server start
        18:42:06.919 SYN Ready: account="bigalon" sid=1, active_sessions=0   <- FIRST join
        18:42:22.209 Auth ACKed
        18:42:46.572 PLAYER63 ChangePlayer [(0, None, 0)]        <- the REMOVE record
        18:42:46.572 TEARDOWN AC2E_Bigalon ... room 45 (re-join: clearing previous presence)
        18:42:46.572 ROOM     AC2E_Bigalon -> room 45 (peers=0, assigned)
      It fired on a first join and named the room being ENTERED. Harmless in that test only
      because obj=none and peers=0. With peers present it would broadcast a msg-63 REMOVE for a
      player who is ARRIVING - producing exactly the invisibility v353 existed to fix, for
      everyone, on every join.

      Disabled with `if False and ...` rather than deleted, so the call site, the deadlock
      constraint and the reasoning stay together for whoever picks this up.

      THE UNDERLYING BUG IS STILL OPEN (ALL41_MAD: exit to HQ, re-join, mutual invisibility -
      see the v353 entry for the full trace). The fix needs a signal that distinguishes
      "presence ALREADY advertised to peers" from "about to be advertised". entered_game is not
      that signal. A dedicated flag set where PLAYER62 announces the join and cleared by
      teardown_ingame_presence and the msg-64 leave path would be - but it MUST be validated
      against a first-join log before being trusted, exactly as this one was caught.

      PROCESS NOTE: v353 shipped with an explicit falsification criterion written into both the
      code comment and the changelog - "if [TEARDOWN] ever appears on a FIRST join, that guard
      assumption is wrong and this must be revisited immediately". The very first test run
      produced that line and the revert took one read. Write the disproof condition down BEFORE
      shipping; it converts an ambiguous test result into a decision.

