v351: [WIRE/CRITICAL] FOUND IT. A multi-record telemetry frame relayed verbatim CTDs every
      recipient. This is the `in 7'165` that has been killing arenas since 26 July.

      v350's instrumentation caught it on the very first run:
        13:23:44.816 TELEM-SIZE Fantum  97B bc=5  T=0xd2 opcode 0x08 obj=0x0100
        13:25:29.294 TELEM-SIZE Fantum 169B bc=10 T=0x52 opcode 0x07 obj=0x0104
        13:25:33.352 TELEM-SIZE Fantum 178B bc=10 T=0xe2 opcode 0x08 obj=0x0104
      bc=10, T=0x52 -> 160 + 5 = 165-byte appspace = the `in 7'165` seen in every crash log.
      That is almost exactly TWO 84-byte records batched into one msg 7.

      PROOF, from the same run:
        13:25:29.294  server relays the 169B frame
        13:25:29.517  AC2E_Bigalon CTD  ARR<NET::OBJECT*,2048>[49382]  (saw 2x `in 7'165`)
        13:25:30.014  Moira@HQ     CTD  ARR<NET::OBJECT*,2048>[49382]  (saw 2x `in 7'165`)
      SAME garbage index on both peers, 720ms apart, from one relayed frame. Fantum - the
      SENDER - received ZERO `in 7'` frames and did not crash, because a client never gets its
      own telemetry back. That is why the originator always survived and the bug kept looking
      like it belonged to whoever was watching.
      Indices seen from this defect across the week: 2144, 2468, 53808, 49382.

      MECHANISM: the receiving client's msg-7 handler walks ONE record. It reads the first,
      then treats the trailing record as an object index and indexes the 2048-slot
      NET::OBJECT ring with it.

      FIX: TELEM_MAX_BC = 5. Refuse to relay any telemetry frame with bc > 5. Every safe frame
      ever observed is bc=5 (one record). Logged once per session under [TELEM-GUARD].
      DROP rather than truncate: the record boundary inside the batch is not established, and
      guessing one reproduces the same crash. A dropped tick is invisible - telemetry runs at
      ~13/s and the next normal frame re-syncs the peer.

      WHY THE TWO EARLIER ATTEMPTS MISSED IT - read this before touching either:
        * v341 (relay over-read) truncates to the length the frame's own header declares. This
          frame is SELF-CONSISTENT: bc=10 genuinely describes 165 bytes, so the guard passed it
          and TELEM-GUARD correctly stayed silent. It is not a buffer over-read.
        * v349 (orphaned bail-out plane) was a real bug and is still fixed, but it was not this
          one: in run_20260727_130625 the despawn went out 0.9s before the crash in the correct
          order (DELETE 0x0101 -> CONFIRM5 -> CREATE2 0x0104) and the peer still died.
      Both were diagnosed from correlation without a controlling case. What finally worked was
      logging the offending frame itself - one instrumentation pass, answer on the first run.

      ALSO LEARNED: TELEM_OPCODES covers 0x07 AND 0x08 (byte[4] of the appspace). The 97B and
      178B frames above are opcode 0x08. Both are relayed by this path.

      OPEN: why Fantum's client batches two records at all - it may be a catch-up burst after
      the STALE-TICK freeze that precedes several of these. If it turns out peers CAN handle a
      batch when every referenced object is known to them, the better fix is to split the frame
      into single records and relay each - but establish the record boundary from FA.exe first,
      do not infer it from lengths.

