v349: [OBJECTS/CRITICAL] a bail-out plane that was never despawned killed every peer in the
      arena. THIS is the object overwrite - a new NetPlane taking a client station that an
      orphaned object still occupied.

      SYMPTOM: AC2E_Bigalon flying; another player joins, bails out, joins again; Bigalon CTDs
      with `in 7'165` then
          Exception in Fighter Ace engine: bounds error
          class ARR<class NET::OBJECT *,2048>[2468] 0..2047

      ROOT CAUSE: bailing leaves the empty plane flying ON PURPOSE (the held kill is credited
      when it comes down), but nothing despawns it if the pilot RE-SPAWNS first. Peers then hold
      TWO live objects on the SAME client station: the new NetPlane overwrites the station
      binding while the old object stays in the 2048-slot NET::OBJECT ring with nothing pointing
      at it. The next update referencing it walks off the end of the ring.
      TIMELINE (server_4_ + messages54, room 53):
        12:46:13.308 Fantum BAILED OUT -> parachuter 0x0105, plane 0x0104 keeps flying
        12:46:33.501 parachuter 0x0105 landed -> deleted
        ...           NO DELETE3 FOR 0x0104, EVER
        12:46:57.728 Fantum re-spawns -> ServerConfirm Number=262 (0x0106)
        12:46:58.275 CREATE2 Fantum St=1 ONumber=0x0106 -> AC2E_Bigalon
        12:46:58.4   Bigalon: "Create NetPlane (Spit-Vb LF). St=1, ONumber=262" -> `in 7'165`
                     -> ARR<NET::OBJECT*,2048>[2468] -> CTD

      NOT v341's BUG - IMPORTANT. The crash signature is identical to the v341 mass-CTD, but
      TELEM-GUARD fired ZERO times in this run, and Bigalon had already survived TWO earlier
      165B frames in the same session. The oversized frame is the TRIGGER; the orphaned object
      is the CAUSE. Do not re-open relay_telemetry on this signature alone - check TELEM-GUARD
      first, then check for a duplicate station binding.

      FIX: record the abandoned plane at bail time (bailed_plane_obj), and at the CONFIRM5 spawn
      site - which runs BEFORE the CREATE2 for the new plane - broadcast a delete for it so the
      station is free when the new NetPlane lands. broadcast_object_delete_3 always addresses
      s.my_obj_number and that is already the NEW number by then, so the abandoned number is
      swapped in for the call and restored in a finally. Safe because that function builds the
      delete packet SYNCHRONOUSLY and the sender thread closes over the finished bytes.

      NEAR MISS WORTH RECORDING: the first attempt passed obj_number_override=... to
      broadcast_object_delete_3. THAT PARAMETER DOES NOT EXIST - the real signature is
      (s, reason, clear_peer_created, followup_pkt, followup_label, killer, exit_byte,
      exit_entry). It would have raised TypeError on EVERY spawn. Read the signature before
      calling into that function; it is the single most load-bearing helper in the file.

      KNOWN GAP, accepted deliberately: the kill held against the abandoned plane can no longer
      be credited once it despawns, because nothing is left to come down. A peer-wide CTD is
      worse than a lost credit. Revisit when the held-kill mechanism is sorted out - the pilot
      already starts a new life when the parachute lands, so the empty plane has no further
      purpose in the current design.

      TEST: two players, one bails and immediately re-spawns without waiting for the empty plane
      to come down. Expect a PARA line naming the despawn, and NO CTD on the observer.

