v347: [RESUPPLY] SUPPLY_GRANT_FLAGS 0x08 -> 0x01. The msg-60 flag bits were mislabelled; the
      repair/rearm branch was unreachable, and our own bit 3 was printing the "shortage"
      messages we had been reading as evidence of a broken economy.

      DECODED FROM THE CLIENT (FA.exe 0x005581c0, the msg-60 handler). Layout confirmed against
      the wire - the handler reads flags at param+3, amount u16 at +4, amount2 u16 at +6, which
      is exactly the 8 bytes the client logs as `in 60'8`:
        bit0 0x01  the branch that formats amounts into messages 0x9f/0xa1, then calls
                   FUN_004ebf00(amount) / FUN_004ec5c0(amount2), FUN_004ebd10(), and sets
                   plane+0x1b4c = 1. That write and FUN_004ebd10 occur NOWHERE ELSE in the
                   handler - unreachable with bit0 clear.
        bit1 0x02  ignore amount,  set fuel to max (plane+0x4e8)
        bit2 0x04  ignore amount2, set ammo to max (plane+0x500)
        bit3 0x08  emit messages 0xe5 and 0xe6 at LAB_005584e5. DISPLAY ONLY - no state change,
                   no economy test anywhere near it.
        bit4 0x10  suppress fuel message;   bit5 0x20  suppress ammo message

      TWO CONSEQUENCES:
        (1) We were sending 0x08, so bit0 was clear and the repair/rearm calls were never
            reached. Fuel arrived; repair and rearm did not. That matches every report.
        (2) "No additional ammunition available" / "Insufficient aircraft units at this base to
            supply your plane normally" are NOT the field economy reporting a shortage. Bit 3 is
            their only gate, so we printed them on every grant regardless of stock. An earlier
            session read them back as proof the economy was empty and nearly went off to fix a
            non-existent bug. 0xa4 additionally fires whenever amount2 == 0.

      HOW THE LABELS GOT WRONG: v285 recorded 0x08 as "incremental top-up + client-side
      base-supply accounting" and it survived ~60 versions unchallenged. Identical failure to
      DEATH_MEC_NIBBLES in v345, where the crash and menu-exit codes were swapped in a comment.
      Both were plausible labels that had never been checked against the binary. When a constant
      encodes client behaviour, decode it from FA.exe - do not infer it from what we intended.

      DIRECTION OF msg 0x49 SETTLED WHILE DOING THIS: the client does NOT request supply. It
      REPLIES. messages47 shows `in 60'8` -> EVENT text -> `out 73'39`, and 73 = 0x49 = the
      SendRepairInfo built at FUN_004c8690 (39-byte body, opcode 0x49, FUN_007e3350(buf,0x27)).
      A test with auto-resupply DISABLED (run_20260726_125335) confirmed it: full sortie,
      landing, engine off, and the client sent nothing but chat. Do not re-run that experiment.

      ALSO CORRECTED: sub=0x53 is NOT engine on/off. ...5300 always precedes a SPAWN, ...5301
      always precedes an EXIT (sub=0x03 or the 0x3a HQ catalog) - verified across three sessions
      and many pilots. So the v339-era rule "engine-ON clears at_airfield" actually fires when
      the pilot climbs OUT, and at_airfield is never cleared during a sortie in ANY arena. The
      v346 note calling this air-start-specific is wrong: the bug was general. It also means
      resupply-after-landing already works on v346, and v346's movement gate is now the ONLY
      thing preventing mid-air rearms.
      STILL BROKEN, and this is what position resolution is actually for: at_airfield records
      where you SPAWNED. Take off from field A, land at field B, and you are supplied from
      field A's stock.

      TEST: fly, land, stop. Expect the two "Insufficient..." lines to DISAPPEAR, new EVENT text
      carrying actual quantities, and a visible repair/rearm rather than fuel only.
      REVERT: SUPPLY_GRANT_FLAGS back to 0x08.

==============================================================================

v346: [RESUPPLY] auto msg-60 rearms were being granted to AIRBORNE aircraft. New ground gate.

      SYMPTOM: pilot reports an in-flight repair while in combat at altitude.

      ROOT CAUSE - two faults compounding:
        (a) at_airfield is set at spawn and cleared ONLY on the engine-ON transition. In an
            AIR-START arena that transition NEVER OCCURS - the pilot spawns with the engine
            already running - so at_airfield stays set for the entire sortie. Confirmed on the
            wire: the only 0x53 messages before those grants carry ...5300 (engine OFF); the
            ...5301 for _AvA_Insane lands at 02:31:22, FIVE MINUTES AFTER his three grants.
        (b) Gates (1)-(4) only prove the position REPEATED, which is also precisely what a
            frozen conductor tick produces - and tick freezes are endemic (6 of 12 pilots).
      Together: stalled telemetry in an air-start room reads as "stationary at an airfield".
      7 grants to airborne aircraft in run_20260726_022244_1_, every one at AF=0, altitudes
      2112-3084, positions scattered across the whole map. Firing a msg-60 in flight is exactly
      what v277 exists to prevent - it rebuilds the plane object and can FREEZE the client.
      NOTE this makes the endemic tick-freeze a CAUSE of a second bug, not just a symptom.

      FIX: new gate (4b) - refuse the auto-grant when plane_movement(s) >= CRASH_MOVEMENT_MIN,
      the same predicate plane_was_flying()/the DEATH classifier already rely on. No new
      threshold was invented and no scene data is needed.

      CALIBRATION (this is why a ground-start sample was requested before writing anything):
        run_20260726_122104, ground start, room 49, AF=2, engine OFF throughout - TWO CORRECT
        grants at pos=(4624,21449,3908) and (4184,18889,3902), and the exit logged
        "exited UNDAMAGED and PARKED (movement=0)".
        Airborne removals log movement=242665..301359.
        So 0 vs ~250k - the existing threshold separates them cleanly.

      REJECTED DESIGN - do not retry it: "clear at_airfield once the plane moves away from its
      spawn position". The legitimate ground session TAXIED 2560 units in Y between its two
      valid grants, so a moved-from-spawn rule would have broken the working case. This was
      caught only because a real ground-start log was obtained first.

      NOT DONE: v339's position-based field resolution from fa_scenes.json is still the eventual
      answer for detecting a LANDING (so a pilot who takes off and returns can resupply again).
      This version stops the harm; it does not restore that capability.

