      22 failures across indexes 0,1,4,7,8,10,11 out of 83 msg-88 arrivals.

      The broadcast fans out to every session in the room with no check that the recipient was
      ever advertised the subject:
          _targets = [t for t in get_sessions_in_room(s.current_room) if t is not s]
      That is CONSISTENT with the symptom but does not establish the cause - the recipient may
      never have been sent a msg 62 for that player, may have been sent one late, or the index
      may have been recycled. Suppressing sends on that guess risks silencing legitimate rank
      announcements, so this version only makes the question answerable: the ACE88 line now
      lists the recipient pilots by name.

      TO CLOSE IT NEXT SESSION: take one failing PlayerIndex from a client log, find the ACE88
      line that carried it, read off which peers received it, and check whether a PLAYER62
      advertisement for that subject ever went to those peers. That is a direct log-to-log
      cross-reference and it decides between "never advertised" and "advertised but stale".
      Precedent: the same move on FLY23 in v343 turned an undiagnosable subsystem into a
      solved one in a single session. Do not guess at a suppression rule before doing this.

      ALSO NOTED: v343 sat on disk UNDEPLOYED for a full session. server_2_.log shows the last
      start banner as v342 (02:22:44) still serving traffic at 07:37 - the file was v343, the
      process was v342, and an entire test session was diagnosed against the wrong build before
      anyone noticed. Check the banner in the run log against VERSION before analysing anything.

      OPEN, DELIBERATELY NOT FIXED HERE: 5 of 6 deaths in the 07:32 session were classified
      "re-fly/plane-swap, NOT a death" because they carried exit=0x11 (MEC nibble 1); the one
      that counted carried exit=0xa0 (nibble 10). The v232 note says a real crash arrives as
