v331: [MP] AIR-TO-AIR COLLISIONS ARE NOW RELAYED - msg 125 (0x7d, CollInfoToPlane).
      Reported as "Snoman collides with Warrior, Snoman detects it, Warrior doesn't".
      The server had NO handler for sub=0x7d at all. It fell through to the generic echo
      path - which is why it only ever appeared tagged SUPPLY-CAP - so the sender got its own
      message bounced back and THE OTHER PLANE WAS NEVER TOLD.
      That is survivable only while BOTH clients happen to geometry-detect the same collision,
      because then each kills itself off its own detection. But the two clients see each other
      through interpolated, network-delayed positions, so routinely only ONE decides the hulls
      intersected - and then the detector explodes while the other flies on unaware.
      EVIDENCE: in run_20260725_135341 at 20:13:14 both DID detect (so that particular one
      worked, mutually and correctly - both scored a kill, both were deleted). Warrior's client
      log still shows the defect plainly: exactly one "out 125'23" and exactly one "in 125'23",
      and the one that came back is HIS OWN ("NumberFrom=259" = Warrior's object). Snoman's 125
      never reached him.
      WIRE FORMAT, decoded from that capture against the client's own line
      "Receive CollInfoToPlane. ExitCode=4, NumberTo=258, NumberFrom=259":
          pl[4]    = 0x7d
          pl[7:9]  = NumberTo   u16 LE - the OTHER plane
          pl[9:11] = NumberFrom u16 LE - the sender's own plane
      Warrior sent To=0x0102 From=0x0103; Snoman sent To=0x0103 From=0x0102 - both matching the
      CREATE2/DELETE3 object numbers for that pair exactly.
      FIX: route by NumberTo to that one peer rather than broadcasting - a collision concerns
      exactly two planes - resolving the target with _peer_owning_object() (v326) so it still
      lands if the target has already respawned onto a new Number.
      Verified by replaying both captured packets through the new routing: each is delivered to
      the other player, and in the asymmetric case the non-detecting client now gets told.

      NOTE ON THE SUPPLIED LOGS: messages14.log (labelled Snoman) covers 19:47:42-19:58:16 and
      the collision was at 20:13:14 - it ends 15 minutes early and contains no collision at all.
      Only Warrior's side (messages67) plus the server log were usable here. Diagnosis did not
      need Snoman's client in the end, but for the asymmetric case it would.

      *** UPDATE - Snoman's real log (messages15.log) arrived and PROVES the asymmetric case. ***
      First, a trap: Snoman's client clock runs ~25s BEHIND Warrior's and the server (his
      "20:12:49.150" detection is the server's 20:13:14.272), and the two clients' in-game
      clocks differ by three hours (04:12pm vs 01:13pm). Correlate on the SERVER log, never on
      client timestamps.
      With that aligned there were TWO collisions, not one:
        #1  server ~20:13:14 - BOTH clients geometry-detected it independently, so both died
            and both scored. It worked, but only by luck.
        #2  server ~20:16:22 (Snoman's clock 20:15:57) - ONLY Snoman detected
            ("Detect collision ... with plane Warrior. Dist=6.954231", ExitCode=0, a glancing
            one). Warrior's log at that exact instant contains nothing but
            "You have hit GBR Cdt Snoman (left wing)" - he was shooting, entirely unaware a
            collision had happened. Warrior's whole session contains exactly ONE
            "Detect collision"; Snoman's contains two.
      And the clincher, across both clients and all three receipts: EVERY CollInfoToPlane
      either client received names ITS OWN object as NumberFrom -
            Warrior  NumberTo=258 NumberFrom=259  (Warrior is 0x0103=259)
            Snoman   NumberTo=259 NumberFrom=258  (Snoman  is 0x0102=258)
            Snoman   NumberTo=260 NumberFrom=257  (Snoman  is 0x0101=257)
      so neither ever received the other's 125; each only ever got its own bounced back. That
      is the bug end to end, on both sides.
      Collision #2 through the new routing: Snoman's packet carries To=0x0104, which is
      Warrior's object at that moment, so _peer_owning_object() resolves it to Warrior and the
      relay lands - the case that was silently dropped before.
      Also worth noting for later: #1 carried ExitCode=4 and #2 ExitCode=0, so that field
      likely distinguishes a fatal collision from a glancing one. Not decoded yet; we relay the
      packet verbatim, so the client decides either way.

