v330: [OPS] FIX v329's update pre-flight - it refused pulls that plain `git pull` does fine.
      Alon's first run aborted with "7 uncommitted local change(s)", but six of the seven were
      UNTRACKED ('??') runtime artifacts - __pycache__/, fa_server.db, logs/, nohup.out,
      server.log, ticket.vr1 - which git does not track and which can never block a pull. Only
      ' M ticket_base.vr1' was a real modification. v329 treated any `git status --porcelain`
      output as dirty and bailed.
      The deeper error was doing the check at all: GIT ALREADY MAKES EXACTLY THE RIGHT CALL.
      `git pull --ff-only` refuses cleanly ("Your local changes would be overwritten by merge",
      rc=1, working tree untouched) if an incoming commit would clobber a local edit, and
      otherwise fast-forwards and leaves the edit in place. The pre-flight was both redundant
      and stricter than git, which is why it blocked what Alon had been doing by hand for weeks.
      NOW: untracked files are ignored (counted at DEBUG only); tracked modifications are listed
      for visibility but do NOT abort; git arbitrates. A failing pull is still a hard abort with
      no restart, and its message now points at the file to commit/stash. UPDATE_ALLOW_DIRTY is
      gone - there is nothing left for it to override.
      Verified live on a real repo: untracked junk + an unrelated tracked edit -> pull succeeds,
      code updates, local edit preserved; remote then touches the same edited file -> rc=1,
      abort, no restart, tree left exactly as it was with nothing half-applied.

==============================================================================

v329: [OPS] SELF-UPDATE - `update` console command + an admin button under the console.
      Runs `git pull --ff-only` in the server directory and then restarts. One implementation:
      the web button just queues the same `update` console command, so both entry points share
      a code path and the progress streams to the web console for free (tag UPDATE).

      RESTART BY EXITING, not by systemctl. Calling `systemctl restart <self>` from inside the
      unit's own process is unreliable - systemd stops the unit, killing the process that issued
      the command, and the restart can race - AND it needs root or a sudoers rule. Exiting needs
      NO privileges and behaves identically under systemd, supervisor, docker and NSSM.
      REQUIREMENT: the unit must have a restart policy. systemd:
          Restart=always
          RestartSec=2
      If yours is Restart=on-failure instead, set UPDATE_EXIT_CODE=1 so the exit looks like a
      failure and still triggers a restart. To drive an external restarter instead, set
      UPDATE_RESTART_MODE='command' + UPDATE_RESTART_COMMAND=['sudo','systemctl','restart','...'].

      PRE-FLIGHT CHECKS - it refuses rather than half-updating:
        * not a git work tree            -> abort
        * uncommitted local edits        -> abort, listing them (UPDATE_ALLOW_DIRTY to override).
          A pull onto a dirty tree either refuses halfway or leaves a merge in progress, and the
          server would then restart into a half-updated directory - far worse than not updating.
        * `--ff-only`                    -> never a merge commit, never a conflicted tree; if the
          branches have diverged it fails cleanly and NOTHING restarts
        * HEAD unchanged                 -> already up to date, no restart
      A concurrent second request is ignored (_update_lock, non-blocking).
      Verified end-to-end against a real repo: clean/ahead -> restart; re-run -> no restart;
      dirty tree -> abort; non-repo dir -> abort.

      SAFETY: every subprocess call passes an argv LIST with no shell, so nothing in this path
      can be command-injected; the button is admin-only (same gate as the console box) and the
      requesting admin is named in the log. Timeouts on every git call.

      ALSO FIXED WHILE ADDING THE BUTTON: LOG_CONSOLE_PAGE is a plain (non-raw) triple-quoted
      string, so a `\n` written into its JavaScript becomes a REAL newline at import time and
      breaks the enclosing JS string literal - taking the whole console page's script block with
      it. The confirm() text needs `\\n`. Caught before shipping; worth remembering, the same
      trap applies to any JS added to that page.

==============================================================================

v328: [PERF] Scaling work ahead of large sessions. Two changes, both measured.
      BACKGROUND - profiling the hot paths against the real packet rate from
      run_20260724_073916 (2.94 inbound pkt/s/player) showed raw CPU is NOT the first limit.
      Relay fan-out is O(N^2) WITHIN an arena: 200 players in one arena is ~117,000 UDP
      sends/s and ~57% of one core, but the same 200 across 8 arenas is ~14%. What falls over
      first is disk I/O and thread pressure, not arithmetic.

      (1) FILE LOG LEVEL NOW DEFAULTS TO INFO (was DEBUG).
      DEBUG writes a line per packet, SYNCHRONOUSLY, on the packet path - 55 MB for a single
      8-player session, and since packet volume is O(N^2) the write volume grows faster than
      linearly. Added fa_logging.set_file_level() + get_levels(), and the console command is
      now `loglevel console|file <LEVEL>` (bare `loglevel` reports both). So per-packet detail
      is one command away for a reproduction run, with no restart, and back off afterwards.
      The WARNING+ error file is deliberately NOT retunable - failures are always captured.
      Worth knowing: the ring buffer feeding the WEB live console is populated BEFORE handler
      filtering, so the on-screen console still shows DEBUG lines while the file sits at INFO -
      live visibility is kept without paying the disk cost.
      TRADE-OFF, stated plainly: at INFO you lose the RX/REL/PL hex dumps, TX/RELIABLE traces
      and RELAY lines - exactly the material that solved v315, v324 and v327. Turn DEBUG back
      on for small reproduction runs.

      (2) THE POST-AUTH DISPATCH LOOP IS EVENT-DRIVEN INSTEAD OF POLLING.
      It used to run `while not s.closing: <drain>; time.sleep(0.02)` - waking 50x/second per
      session whether or not anything had arrived. Measured at 200 sessions: ~9,955 wakeups/s
      and 8.8% of a core with ZERO traffic. It now waits on a per-session Event, signalled by
      the RX path when a command is queued: ~388 wakeups/s and 3.6% at the same 200 sessions.
      A plain Event is used ON PURPOSE rather than a Condition on s._lock: it shares no lock
      with anything else, so it cannot deadlock against the four existing `with s._lock:` sites.
      ORDER IS wait -> clear -> drain. Clearing BEFORE draining means a command queued in the
      gap is still caught by that same drain, and one landing after it leaves the event set so
      the next pass wakes immediately - a command can never be stranded, the worst case being
      one harmless empty wakeup. The 0.5s timeout is only a backstop bounding how long
      s.closing goes unnoticed at shutdown.
      Latency IMPROVES: a queued command now wakes the thread immediately instead of waiting
      out the rest of a 20ms sleep.
      VERIFIED: 41,000 messages across 1 / 8 / 200 concurrent producers, zero lost, zero
      duplicated.

      NOT DONE, deliberately: the thread-per-recipient broadcast pattern (one OS thread per
      recipient per chat line - 200 threads and ~23ms at 200 players) is still there. It wants
      a small worker pool, which is a bigger change than these two and was left for its own
      pass. Arena population is NOT capped, per Alon - the game itself handles 300+, so the
      scaling answer there is a separate high-capacity arena server rather than a limit.

==============================================================================

v327: [MP] *** PREFIXED MESSAGES SOLVED - AND PLAYER CHAT WAS BEING SILENTLY DROPPED. ***
      The 257-per-peak-window "PREFIXED message we are NOT re-framing" notes are gone, and the
      cause turned out to be much more mundane - and more damaging - than a framing mystery.

      LAYOUT, now PROVEN on 100+ real samples (not inferred):
          pl[0]   = 0x00              always
          pl[1]   = per-session message counter
          pl[2:4] = cmd, BE u16       non-zero == this is the prefixed form
          pl[4:]  = a NORMAL appspace frame [bc][T][00][00][sub][payload]
                    with Size = bc*16 + (T>>4) == the real payload length, on every sample.
      (So POST-AUTH's "type=0x.." on these lines was never a type - it is pl[1], the counter.)
      Confirmed from the client side too: the game hands raw buffers to vcncSendMessage()
      (ConductorInterface.cpp, FUN_007e6f60 -> FUN_007e6180), so the 4-byte prefix is VNET
      transport framing, not something the game builds.

      THE ACTUAL BUG: the real sub sits at pl[8], and almost every handler ALREADY reads pl[8]
      for exactly this shape - the "type-scan double-wrap" fallbacks for chat 0x14, team-select
      0x44, leave 0x40, delete 0x03, 0x17, 0x1f, plus the whole scan-inner block. They were just
      gated on `sub == 0x00`. For a prefixed message sub = pl[4] = the inner bc, and bc is 0 only
      while the payload is <= 15 bytes. So SHORT messages were delivered and LONGER ONES WERE
      SILENTLY DISCARDED. Measured on real chat from the run:
          "~S~"                             bc=0  -> delivered
          "hey Butterz ~S~"                 bc=1  -> DROPPED
          "Good to see old names back in"   bc=2  -> DROPPED
          "Yeah, LOL, been doing that ..."  bc=3  -> DROPPED
      Named chat (0xcd) always worked only because its branch tests pl[8] without consulting sub
      at all - which is exactly why nobody spotted that plain chat had a length cliff.
      Real messages lost in run_20260724_073916 include "Good to see old names back in",
      "hey Butterz ~S~", "Moira@HQ: Hi guys" and "Taurus: Hiya Warrior".

      FIX (PREFIXED_NORMALISE_SUB): for a prefixed message, normalise sub to 0x00 so those
      existing, already-correct pl[8] handlers fire for a payload of ANY length. Deliberately
      NOT a blanket re-frame: the bytes are untouched, so nothing that reads fixed pl[..]
      offsets can shift underneath it, and any sub with no handler stays ignored exactly as
      before. The delete-notify (pl[8]==0x03) keeps its existing proven re-frame path.
      SAFETY - the v246 worry was that switching these on would fire something dangerous (one
      decodes to 0xd4 = LEAVE). It cannot: the blanket echo fall-through is inside `if cmd == 0:`
      (line ~11298) and every pl[8] handler sits BEFORE it, so a prefixed message can reach a
      handler but can never reach the echo. Verified by replaying real captured payloads through
      the dispatch chain: long chat recovered, and every message type that already worked routes
      to exactly the same place as before.
===========================
v326: [MP] PEER DELETES FOR A JUST-RESPAWNED PLANE WERE SWALLOWED, AND THE RE-ARM WITH THEM.
      Second bug found while opening the PREFIXED delete-notify task, and independent of it.
      The delete-notify ownership lookup compared `_onum` against each peer's CURRENT
      my_obj_number only. A peer that had respawned since the delete was sent no longer matched,
      so the message was logged "neither its own nor any peer's" and dropped.
      The DROP is harmless on its own - that object really is gone. What is NOT harmless is that
      the same branch performs the `_created_peers.discard(sender)` RE-ARM, which is what makes
      the telemetry relay re-create that peer's plane for the sender. Miss the lookup and the
      re-arm never happens, so THE SENDER NEVER SEES THAT PEER AGAIN. That is "players not
      seeing each other", by a different route from v324.
      These are all from run_20260724_073916, and every one carries a REAL Number, not garbage:
          02:32:45  ALL41_MAD  delete for 0x0107  (Snoman's, freed 28s earlier)
          02:50:29  Taurus     delete for 0x0105  (Sparky's)
          02:50:30  Warrior    delete for 0x0105  (Sparky's)
          02:48:26  Warrior    delete for 0x0101
      FIX: each session now keeps a bounded history of every Number it has worn
      (_obj_number_history, last 16, stamped with issue time), and _peer_owning_object() falls
      back to it when no current Number matches. The grace window is set EQUAL to
      OBJ_NUMBER_QUARANTINE_SEC, so a historical hit is provably unambiguous - inside that window
      v324 guarantees the Number cannot have been re-issued to anyone else, and outside it we
      decline to guess. Resolutions via history are tagged in the log.

      THE PREFIXED DELETE-NOTIFY TASK ITSELF IS STILL OPEN. What is established so far:
        * `cmd` is a BIG-ENDIAN u16 at pl[2:4] (parser: struct.unpack_from('>H', pl, 2)).
          cmd == 0 is the normal case; cmd != 0 means a 4-byte prefix sits in front of the real
          reliable frame, so the real [bc][T][00][00][sub] starts at offset 4.
        * pl[0:2] looks like a monotonic per-connection counter - observed 0x0000, 0x0003,
          0x0005, 0x0007, 0x0008, 0x000a, 0x000d, 0x0014, 0x0015, 0x001b, 0x001d, 0x001e,
          0x001f, 0x0025 across one session.
        * handle_post_auth re-frames ONLY when pl[8] == 0x03 (delete-notify). Every other
          prefixed message is logged and dropped - 257 in the peak window alone.
        * Blanket re-framing is NOT safe: one of the currently-ignored subs decodes to 0xd4 =
          LEAVE (noted in v246), so switching them all on at once would break the lobby.
      WHAT IS NEEDED: the actual byte layout, from either (a) the RX/REL/PL hex that already sits
      two lines below each REFRAME line in the run log, or (b) the client's reliable send path in
      Ghidra. Only two hand-written examples exist in the v246 comment and they disagree about
      whether pl[2:4] mirrors the inner [bc][T], so this must be settled on real data rather than
      inferred.
===========================
